INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - CONVERTED SKI LODGE - NIGHT

A great timber hall with a stone hearth, now lined with cots. Antlers still hang over the mantel. Blizzard wind works the shutters.

DR. SAOIRSE KELLEHER, 40, mud on her boots and blood on her cuffs, stands over a cot with a stethoscope draped around her neck. She has not slept in two days and she holds her face carefully, like something fragile on a tray.

NURSE ABIODUN, 30, sets out instruments on a ski-rack trolley. He works precisely, wasting no motion.

On the cot, MRS. HELENA PAVLICEK, 70, lies pale under three blankets, her breath shallow. At the foot of the bed, her husband GREGOR PAVLICEK, 72, holds his hat in both hands, turning it by the brim.

SAOIRSE
(quietly)
Mr. Pavlicek. The avalanche took her ribs, and one of them has nicked the lung.

GREGOR
She was talking on the way down.

SAOIRSE
I know.

She checks the pulse at Helena's wrist. She counts, and he waits, and she finds nothing to say about the number.

GREGOR
You'll operate.

SAOIRSE
We would have to open the chest. I have a surgical kit and a lantern. The helicopter can't land for another night.

GREGOR
Then do it.

ABIODUN
Doctor, the generator's down to an hour.

She glances at him. He looks back, steady. In that look is a whole second conversation.

SAOIRSE
Then we have an hour.

GREGOR
I said do it.

SAOIRSE
I heard you.

She turns, takes a scalpel from the trolley, and holds it up to the light. Her hand is perfectly steady.

Gregor takes a step forward. He sets his hat on the bed. His knuckles are white.

GREGOR
And if you cut and she dies on your table?

SAOIRSE
Then she dies knowing someone tried.

GREGOR
Then I forbid it.

ABIODUN
Sir.

GREGOR
She is mine. Fifty years. I will not have her opened by a stranger in a dead man's hunting lodge.

He grabs Saoirse's wrist. The scalpel stops in the air. Abiodun moves in, but she shakes her head, and he freezes.

SAOIRSE
(low)
Mr. Pavlicek. Look at her.

He does not want to. He does. Helena's eyes are open, and they have found him. Her lips move.

HELENA
(a thread of voice)
Greg. Let go.

His grip loosens. The scalpel lowers. The wind slams the shutters, and somewhere the generator stutters.
